WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.920
Welcome to Hydrocarbons.

00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:06.620
This recorded Chemistry class uses an AI generated Marin voice.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:10.160
A hydrocarbon contains only carbon and hydrogen.

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:13.940
Start with connectivity before choosing a reaction.

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:19.520
An acyclic saturated alkane has two more hydrogen atoms than twice its carbon

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:19.860
count.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:25.500
One ring or one double bond removes two hydrogens relative to that saturated

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:28.460
reference; one triple bond removes four.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:32.260
The degree of unsaturation counts rings and pi bonds.

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:37.160
For a neutral ordinary hydrocarbon, take twice the carbon count plus two,

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:40.060
subtract the hydrogen count, and divide by two.

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:46.440
Benzene's formula gives four, consistent with one ring and three pi bonds in a

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:46.880
conventional contributor.

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:52.740
The formula alone does not prove benzene: other connectivities can share it.

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:57.740
Butane has zero degrees, butene has one, and butyne has two.

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:02.440
In but two ene, each double bonded carbon carries two different groups,

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:04.320
permitting geometrical isomers.

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:09.280
A terminal double bond with two hydrogens on one carbon does not meet that

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:09.760
requirement.

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:15.500
Check valence, hydrogen count and possible stereoisomers before interpreting a

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:17.080
formula as one unique molecule.

00:01:17.304 --> 00:01:21.684
Rotation about ethane's carbon carbon single bond changes its conformation

00:01:21.684 --> 00:01:24.104
without changing which atoms are connected.

00:01:24.944 --> 00:01:28.948
In a Newman view along that bond, an eclipsed arrangement aligns front and

00:01:28.948 --> 00:01:29.164
back

00:01:29.164 --> 00:01:32.104
bonds, while a staggered arrangement offsets them.

00:01:32.784 --> 00:01:35.384
Staggered ethane has lower energy in the introductory model.

00:01:36.164 --> 00:01:39.364
These are conformations, not different constitutional isomers.

00:01:40.264 --> 00:01:44.304
Alkane chlorination under light changes connectivity through a radical chain.

00:01:45.064 --> 00:01:49.244
Initiation breaks a chlorine molecule homolytically to form two chlorine

00:01:49.244 --> 00:01:50.184
radicals.

00:01:50.564 --> 00:01:54.624
In propagation, a chlorine radical removes hydrogen from methane to give

00:01:54.624 --> 00:01:56.664
hydrogen chloride and a methyl radical.

00:01:57.704 --> 00:02:01.187
That methyl radical reacts with another chlorine molecule to form

00:02:01.187 --> 00:02:01.884
chloromethane

00:02:01.884 --> 00:02:03.864
and regenerate a chlorine radical.

00:02:04.744 --> 00:02:08.184
Each propagation sequence therefore maintains a reactive radical.

00:02:09.064 --> 00:02:12.104
Termination joins two radicals without generating another.

00:02:12.744 --> 00:02:16.704
Repeated substitution can form products beyond the first chlorinated compound,

00:02:17.224 --> 00:02:20.484
and unsymmetrical alkanes can give several positions of substitution.

00:02:21.644 --> 00:02:24.844
Product amounts depend on both how many hydrogens are available and their

00:02:24.844 --> 00:02:25.924
relative reaction rates.

00:02:26.664 --> 00:02:31.024
Do not predict product percentages from radical stability alone or assume all

00:02:31.024 --> 00:02:33.064
halogenations have identical selectivity.

00:02:33.624 --> 00:02:37.784
An alkene's pi electrons can begin bonding to an electrophile.

00:02:38.504 --> 00:02:43.153
In ordinary ionic addition of hydrogen bromide to propene, protonation can

00:02:43.153 --> 00:02:43.404
lead

00:02:43.404 --> 00:02:46.484
toward a secondary or a primary carbocation.

00:02:47.344 --> 00:02:52.284
The pathway involving the more stable secondary intermediate is favoured, and

00:02:52.284 --> 00:02:54.944
bromide capture gives mainly two bromopropane.

00:02:55.444 --> 00:02:59.524
This is the familiar Markovnikov orientation for this substrate and these

00:02:59.524 --> 00:02:59.984
conditions.

00:02:59.984 --> 00:03:04.864
It is a result of a mechanism, not a direction rule independent of reagents.

00:03:05.864 --> 00:03:09.824
Carbocation rearrangements can change products in substrates where a more

00:03:09.824 --> 00:03:11.524
favourable intermediate is accessible.

00:03:12.624 --> 00:03:16.684
Suitable peroxide initiated radical conditions change the hydrogen bromide

00:03:16.684 --> 00:03:17.104
pathway.

00:03:18.084 --> 00:03:21.828
Bromine radical addition that forms the more stable carbon radical leads,

00:03:21.828 --> 00:03:22.084
after

00:03:22.084 --> 00:03:25.144
hydrogen abstraction, mainly to one bromopropane.

00:03:25.644 --> 00:03:30.624
The standard peroxide reversal applies to hydrogen bromide; it must not be

00:03:30.624 --> 00:03:34.044
transferred automatically to hydrogen chloride or hydrogen iodide.

00:03:34.724 --> 00:03:39.444
For every prediction write the alkene, reagent and radical or ionic conditions

00:03:39.444 --> 00:03:39.884
together.

00:03:40.544 --> 00:03:45.065
The carbon skeleton has three carbons in both propene products; the location

00:03:45.065 --> 00:03:45.184
of

00:03:45.184 --> 00:03:48.264
bromine changes, while no carbon is gained or lost.

00:03:48.624 --> 00:03:53.344
Adding hydrogen to an alkene with an appropriate metal catalyst converts its

00:03:53.344 --> 00:03:54.944
double bond into a single bond.

00:03:55.924 --> 00:03:59.727
Ethene becomes ethane, consuming one mole of hydrogen per mole of double

00:03:59.727 --> 00:04:00.044
bonds.

00:04:01.284 --> 00:04:05.804
Alkene bromination in a suitable non nucleophilic medium commonly forms a

00:04:05.804 --> 00:04:09.184
vicinal dibromide, with bromine atoms on adjacent carbons.

00:04:10.304 --> 00:04:14.424
A bridged intermediate explains the usual anti addition rather than a freely

00:04:14.424 --> 00:04:15.964
rotating carbocation picture.

00:04:16.734 --> 00:04:21.164
If water is present, it can compete as a nucleophile and change the product

00:04:21.164 --> 00:04:22.184
toward a bromohydrin.

00:04:23.404 --> 00:04:27.534
Therefore a reagent name without solvent information may not fully specify the

00:04:27.534 --> 00:04:28.064
chemistry.

00:04:29.204 --> 00:04:33.264
Acid catalysed hydration of propene gives mainly propan two ol through the

00:04:33.264 --> 00:04:34.524
usual ionic orientation.

00:04:35.684 --> 00:04:39.324
Simply mixing an alkene with water does not imply rapid hydration without

00:04:39.324 --> 00:04:40.224
suitable conditions.

00:04:41.064 --> 00:04:46.044
In addition polymerization, many ethene molecules join as the pi bonds are

00:04:46.044 --> 00:04:48.044
replaced by bonds along a saturated chain.

00:04:49.084 --> 00:04:53.044
The polyethene repeat unit contains two carbon atoms each bearing two

00:04:53.044 --> 00:04:53.564
hydrogens.

00:04:55.064 --> 00:04:57.224
Distinguish a repeat unit from a whole polymer molecule.

00:04:58.544 --> 00:05:03.084
Ozonolysis is useful for locating an alkene double bond when the work up is

00:05:03.084 --> 00:05:03.524
specified.

00:05:04.384 --> 00:05:08.444
Under reductive work up, split the double bond and convert each of its carbons

00:05:08.444 --> 00:05:09.644
into a carbonyl carbon.

00:05:10.804 --> 00:05:12.484
Keep the other groups attached to that carbon.

00:05:13.164 --> 00:05:17.623
For but two ene, each double bonded carbon carries hydrogen and methyl, so

00:05:17.623 --> 00:05:17.864
each

00:05:17.864 --> 00:05:20.884
fragment is acetaldehyde, a two carbon aldehyde.

00:05:21.384 --> 00:05:24.204
One original molecule gives two acetaldehyde molecules.

00:05:24.844 --> 00:05:29.184
This result identifies the connectivity but does not by itself distinguish the

00:05:29.184 --> 00:05:31.124
geometrical isomers of but two ene.

00:05:32.164 --> 00:05:36.144
For two methylpropene, one double bonded carbon bears two methyl groups and

00:05:36.144 --> 00:05:39.744
gives propanone; the terminal carbon bears two hydrogens and gives

00:05:39.744 --> 00:05:42.464
formaldehyde, a one carbon aldehyde.

00:05:42.644 --> 00:05:46.424
Add the fragment carbon counts: three plus one returns the original four.

00:05:47.024 --> 00:05:50.764
A double bonded carbon with no hydrogen commonly yields a ketone fragment.

00:05:51.064 --> 00:05:55.304
A carbon bearing hydrogen can yield an aldehyde under the stated reductive

00:05:55.304 --> 00:05:55.824
conditions.

00:05:56.764 --> 00:06:00.844
Oxidative work up can further change aldehyde products, so it must not be

00:06:00.844 --> 00:06:01.744
treated as interchangeable.

00:06:02.704 --> 00:06:07.024
Work backward by reconnecting the two carbonyl carbons when reconstructing a

00:06:07.024 --> 00:06:07.424
possible alkene.

00:06:07.704 --> 00:06:12.564
A terminal alkyne has hydrogen directly attached to a carbon of the triple

00:06:12.564 --> 00:06:12.824
bond.

00:06:13.864 --> 00:06:16.464
Removing this hydrogen gives an acetylide ion.

00:06:17.324 --> 00:06:21.504
The greater s character of an sp orbital stabilizes the negative charge

00:06:22.004 --> 00:06:27.104
compared with comparable simple sp two or sp three carbanions.

00:06:27.104 --> 00:06:31.184
Terminal alkynes are consequently more acidic than comparable alkenes and

00:06:31.184 --> 00:06:34.284
alkanes, while remaining much weaker acids than water.

00:06:34.884 --> 00:06:40.044
A sufficiently strong base such as sodium amide can form an acetylide;

00:06:40.664 --> 00:06:43.504
aqueous hydroxide is not a quantitative substitute.

00:06:44.564 --> 00:06:49.144
Acetylides can form carbon carbon bonds with suitable unhindered primary alkyl

00:06:49.144 --> 00:06:53.036
halides by substitution, while more hindered substrates can favour

00:06:53.036 --> 00:06:53.744
elimination.

00:06:55.064 --> 00:06:59.504
Complete catalytic hydrogenation of an alkyne consumes two equivalents of

00:06:59.504 --> 00:07:01.144
hydrogen to reach an alkane.

00:07:01.904 --> 00:07:06.444
Selective partial hydrogenation with a Lindlar catalyst can give a cis alkene

00:07:06.444 --> 00:07:08.544
from an appropriate internal alkyne.

00:07:09.964 --> 00:07:12.964
Dissolving metal reduction can instead give the trans alkene.

00:07:14.204 --> 00:07:17.804
The alkene formed from a terminal triple bond has a carbon bearing two

00:07:17.804 --> 00:07:21.004
hydrogens, so cis and trans labels do not apply.

00:07:22.004 --> 00:07:25.244
Reagents and equivalents decide how far the reduction proceeds.

00:07:25.416 --> 00:07:30.036
Benzene is a planar cyclic conjugated system with six pi electrons.

00:07:30.856 --> 00:07:35.256
It satisfies the introductory four n plus two aromatic electron rule with n

00:07:35.256 --> 00:07:35.936
equal to one.

00:07:36.796 --> 00:07:41.196
Electron count alone is insufficient: a molecule must also have the required

00:07:41.196 --> 00:07:43.836
cyclic conjugation and appropriate geometry.

00:07:45.116 --> 00:07:49.056
Benzene's carbon carbon bonds are equivalent; alternating bond drawings are

00:07:49.056 --> 00:07:50.836
contributors to a delocalized structure.

00:07:51.236 --> 00:07:55.736
In common electrophilic aromatic substitution, the ring forms a new bond to an

00:07:55.736 --> 00:07:58.916
electrophile and temporarily loses aromaticity.

00:07:59.196 --> 00:08:01.916
Removal of a proton restores the conjugated ring.

00:08:02.676 --> 00:08:06.571
Bromination commonly uses bromine with a Lewis acid such as iron three

00:08:06.571 --> 00:08:07.016
bromide.

00:08:07.676 --> 00:08:10.656
Nitration uses conditions that generate the nitronium electrophile.

00:08:11.456 --> 00:08:15.296
These ring substitutions replace a ring hydrogen and retain the six carbon

00:08:15.296 --> 00:08:15.776
framework.

00:08:16.356 --> 00:08:20.016
They differ from radical substitution on an alkyl side chain under light.

00:08:20.936 --> 00:08:24.956
In toluene, identify whether conditions target the aromatic ring or the

00:08:24.956 --> 00:08:26.936
benzylic position before assigning a product.

00:08:28.216 --> 00:08:31.110
Treat aromatic stabilization as an explanation for common reaction

00:08:31.110 --> 00:08:31.636
preferences,

00:08:32.356 --> 00:08:35.936
rather than a claim that an aromatic ring can never undergo any addition

00:08:35.936 --> 00:08:36.376
reaction.

00:08:36.768 --> 00:08:42.068
A substituent influences both how rapidly an aromatic ring reacts and where a

00:08:42.068 --> 00:08:43.108
new group is introduced.

00:08:44.068 --> 00:08:47.728
An alkyl group generally activates the ring and favours ortho and para

00:08:47.728 --> 00:08:48.348
substitution.

00:08:49.308 --> 00:08:53.008
A nitro group deactivates the ring and favours meta substitution.

00:08:54.048 --> 00:08:58.408
Halogens are an important mixed case: they deactivate overall through electron

00:08:58.408 --> 00:09:02.448
withdrawal but direct toward ortho and para positions through resonance

00:09:02.448 --> 00:09:02.888
donation.

00:09:03.788 --> 00:09:07.488
A directing preference does not promise one pure positional product.

00:09:08.508 --> 00:09:12.428
Friedel Crafts alkylation introduces an alkyl group under suitable Lewis acid

00:09:12.428 --> 00:09:16.808
conditions, but rearrangements and repeated alkylation can complicate the

00:09:16.808 --> 00:09:17.048
result.

00:09:18.448 --> 00:09:22.168
Acylation introduces an acyl group and avoids the analogous carbocation

00:09:22.168 --> 00:09:23.408
skeleton rearrangement.

00:09:24.668 --> 00:09:28.388
Strongly deactivated rings and catalyst binding groups can prevent the usual

00:09:28.388 --> 00:09:31.688
reaction, so do not apply the name without checking the substrate.

00:09:32.248 --> 00:09:37.108
In a short synthesis, mark the reaction centre, count carbons, state the

00:09:37.108 --> 00:09:39.948
reagent and inspect competing pathways at each arrow.

00:09:41.108 --> 00:09:44.348
Use the practice questions to connect these choices rather than memorizing

00:09:44.348 --> 00:09:45.268
product names alone.

00:09:46.108 --> 00:09:49.908
More preparation routes and detailed stereochemical drawings remain useful.

00:09:50.232 --> 00:09:54.932
Alkyne hydration usually requires following an initially formed enol to its

00:09:54.932 --> 00:09:55.612
carbonyl tautomer.

00:09:56.192 --> 00:10:00.732
An enol has hydroxyl attached to an alkene carbon; the final carbonyl product

00:10:00.732 --> 00:10:03.832
is not obtained by simply leaving that double bond unchanged.

00:10:04.632 --> 00:10:09.412
For propyne, aqueous acid with mercury two catalysis gives the Markovnikov

00:10:09.412 --> 00:10:13.072
hydration pattern and, after tautomerization, propanone.

00:10:13.912 --> 00:10:18.572
A suitable bulky borane followed by alkaline peroxide oxidation instead gives

00:10:18.572 --> 00:10:23.292
the terminal alkyne the complementary hydration orientation, leading to the

00:10:23.292 --> 00:10:28.272
aldehyde C H three, C H two, C H O, also called propionaldehyde.

00:10:29.112 --> 00:10:31.192
Both products retain the three carbon skeleton.

00:10:31.972 --> 00:10:35.872
They have the same molecular formula but different carbonyl positions and

00:10:35.872 --> 00:10:36.672
functional classes.

00:10:37.692 --> 00:10:41.712
Ethyne is an important special case: its acid catalysed hydration gives the

00:10:41.712 --> 00:10:46.932
aldehyde C H three, C H O, also called acetaldehyde, rather than a methyl

00:10:46.932 --> 00:10:47.352
ketone.

00:10:48.032 --> 00:10:52.972
Internal unsymmetrical alkynes can give product mixtures, so a terminal alkyne

00:10:52.972 --> 00:10:55.292
shortcut must not be applied indiscriminately.

00:10:56.392 --> 00:10:58.732
Read the catalyst and workup before selecting a product.

00:10:59.752 --> 00:11:04.112
Tautomerization moves a proton and changes bond placement; it is not merely

00:11:04.112 --> 00:11:06.792
resonance between drawings with the same hydrogen attachments.

00:11:07.752 --> 00:11:10.992
This distinction connects hydrocarbon reactions with the carbonyl chemistry

00:11:10.992 --> 00:11:13.012
developed in the Organic Oxygen class.

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:16.880
Reductive ozonolysis can be read backward as well as forward.

00:11:17.800 --> 00:11:21.320
Each carbon of the original double bond becomes a carbonyl carbon while

00:11:21.320 --> 00:11:23.000
retaining its attached carbon groups.

00:11:24.040 --> 00:11:28.405
Suppose the products are one molecule of propanone and one of acetaldehyde,

00:11:28.405 --> 00:11:28.580
the

00:11:28.580 --> 00:11:32.080
two carbon aldehyde C H three, C H O.

00:11:32.080 --> 00:11:34.600
Propanone's carbonyl carbon carries two methyl groups.

00:11:35.220 --> 00:11:38.800
This aldehyde's carbonyl carbon carries one methyl group and one hydrogen.

00:11:40.120 --> 00:11:43.900
Join those two carbonyl carbons with a double bond and remove the carbonyl

00:11:43.900 --> 00:11:45.020
oxygens in the reconstruction.

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:48.360
The original alkene is two methylbut two ene.

00:11:48.940 --> 00:11:52.440
It contains five carbons, matching three plus two in the products.

00:11:53.380 --> 00:11:57.600
This reconstruction determines connectivity, but carbonyl fragments alone do

00:11:57.600 --> 00:12:00.720
not generally recover an alkene's original E or Z geometry.

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:05.640
In this particular alkene, two identical methyl groups on one double bonded

00:12:05.640 --> 00:12:07.960
carbon prevent E and Z isomerism.

00:12:08.700 --> 00:12:13.158
The workup matters: oxidative ozonolysis can further oxidize aldehyde

00:12:13.158 --> 00:12:13.740
products,

00:12:14.460 --> 00:12:15.980
changing the evidence used for reconstruction.

00:12:17.320 --> 00:12:20.440
State reductive conditions before treating an aldehyde as the final fragment.

00:12:21.420 --> 00:12:24.920
Finish by counting carbon atoms and checking which double bonded carbon had a

00:12:24.920 --> 00:12:25.140
hydrogen.

00:12:25.464 --> 00:12:29.844
Aromaticity in the simple Hückel model requires more than a special electron

00:12:29.844 --> 00:12:30.184
count.

00:12:30.904 --> 00:12:35.458
The ring must be cyclic, approximately planar and continuously conjugated,

00:12:35.458 --> 00:12:35.704
with

00:12:35.704 --> 00:12:37.824
a suitable p orbital at each position.

00:12:38.684 --> 00:12:42.864
A closed shell ring with four n plus two pi electrons can then be aromatic.

00:12:43.924 --> 00:12:47.384
Benzene has six pi electrons, corresponding to n equal to one.

00:12:48.064 --> 00:12:53.464
The cyclopentadienyl anion also has six: four from two double bonds and two

00:12:53.464 --> 00:12:55.924
from the relevant lone pair in its conjugated ring.

00:12:56.884 --> 00:13:01.644
The cycloheptatrienyl cation has six pi electrons from three double bonds and

00:13:01.644 --> 00:13:04.064
an empty p orbital at the cationic position.

00:13:05.244 --> 00:13:07.464
Charge alone therefore does not prohibit aromaticity.

00:13:08.304 --> 00:13:12.784
A planar fully conjugated four pi electron ring instead fits the antiaromatic

00:13:12.784 --> 00:13:13.184
pattern.

00:13:13.784 --> 00:13:17.924
A nonplanar molecule that avoids continuous effective overlap may be

00:13:17.924 --> 00:13:22.484
nonaromatic rather than antiaromatic, even if a naive count gives four n

00:13:22.484 --> 00:13:22.904
electrons.

00:13:22.904 --> 00:13:26.464
Cyclooctatetraene is the familiar nonplanar example.

00:13:27.624 --> 00:13:31.284
Always check geometry and conjugation before applying the electron rule.

00:13:32.084 --> 00:13:35.984
The model here concerns simple monocyclic systems and is not a universal

00:13:35.984 --> 00:13:39.364
classification shortcut for every fused or unusual structure.

00:13:40.272 --> 00:13:45.270
An existing substituent affects both how fast an aromatic ring reacts and

00:13:45.270 --> 00:13:45.612
where

00:13:45.612 --> 00:13:47.172
a new group preferentially enters.

00:13:47.932 --> 00:13:48.992
Those are separate questions.

00:13:49.952 --> 00:13:54.072
Relative to carbon one bearing the substituent, carbons two and six are ortho,

00:13:54.472 --> 00:13:57.652
three and five are meta, and carbon four is para.

00:13:58.312 --> 00:14:02.092
A methyl group commonly activates the ring toward electrophilic substitution

00:14:02.092 --> 00:14:04.532
and favours ortho and para products.

00:14:05.052 --> 00:14:09.112
A nitro group strongly deactivates and favours meta substitution.

00:14:10.212 --> 00:14:14.192
Halogens are the important combination: they deactivate the ring overall but

00:14:14.192 --> 00:14:16.492
still direct predominantly ortho and para.

00:14:16.912 --> 00:14:21.132
Their inductive withdrawal and resonance donation influence different aspects

00:14:21.132 --> 00:14:21.872
of the comparison.

00:14:22.752 --> 00:14:26.452
Do not infer that an ortho para director must always make the ring more

00:14:26.452 --> 00:14:27.412
reactive than benzene.

00:14:28.112 --> 00:14:30.712
Nor does directing preference promise one pure product.

00:14:31.312 --> 00:14:35.870
Steric effects, electrophile, medium and additional substituents can affect

00:14:35.870 --> 00:14:36.052
the

00:14:36.052 --> 00:14:36.432
product mixture.

00:14:37.132 --> 00:14:40.552
The labelled rings show relative positions rather than exact yields.

00:14:41.252 --> 00:14:44.972
For several substituents, compare their competing effects and blocked sites

00:14:44.972 --> 00:14:46.512
before predicting the next position.

00:14:47.392 --> 00:14:51.117
Keep electrophilic ring substitution distinct from radical reaction at an

00:14:51.117 --> 00:14:51.372
alkyl

00:14:51.372 --> 00:14:51.812
side chain.

00:14:52.176 --> 00:14:55.776
The benzylic carbon is directly attached to an aromatic ring.

00:14:56.556 --> 00:15:00.236
Its chemistry differs from that of a carbon bearing hydrogen within the ring

00:15:00.236 --> 00:15:00.716
itself.

00:15:01.636 --> 00:15:06.488
Under the usual strong side chain oxidation conditions, an alkylbenzene with

00:15:06.488 --> 00:15:06.616
at

00:15:06.616 --> 00:15:10.976
least one benzylic hydrogen can yield a carboxyl group at the ring attachment,

00:15:11.776 --> 00:15:12.816
followed by acid workup.

00:15:13.716 --> 00:15:18.096
Toluene and ethylbenzene therefore both give benzoic acid in that introductory

00:15:18.096 --> 00:15:18.616
comparison.

00:15:19.416 --> 00:15:24.296
The extra carbon in ethylbenzene does not become a longer carboxylic acid side

00:15:24.296 --> 00:15:24.396
chain.

00:15:25.456 --> 00:15:29.336
Carbon lost from the side chain must be accounted for in a full balanced

00:15:29.336 --> 00:15:34.156
oxidation, rather than silently assumed to remain in the main organic product.

00:15:34.156 --> 00:15:39.436
Tert butylbenzene has no hydrogen on its benzylic carbon and does not follow

00:15:39.436 --> 00:15:41.836
this ordinary side chain oxidation shortcut.

00:15:42.916 --> 00:15:47.236
Radical halogenation under suitable light or radical initiating conditions can

00:15:47.236 --> 00:15:52.016
also target benzylic positions, whereas electrophilic aromatic halogenation

00:15:52.016 --> 00:15:54.756
uses a different pathway and catalyst context.

00:15:55.856 --> 00:15:59.656
Identify the reacting carbon, the presence of its hydrogen and the reagent

00:15:59.656 --> 00:16:03.136
conditions before deciding whether the ring or side chain changes.

00:16:03.336 --> 00:16:08.216
Molecular formula and hydrogen uptake provide complementary evidence, rather

00:16:08.216 --> 00:16:10.056
than a unique structure by themselves.

00:16:11.016 --> 00:16:15.776
For an ordinary neutral hydrocarbon with six carbons and ten hydrogens, the

00:16:15.776 --> 00:16:17.696
degree of unsaturation is two.

00:16:18.416 --> 00:16:23.276
One ring and one double bond, two double bonds, or one triple bond can each

00:16:23.276 --> 00:16:24.096
account for that value.

00:16:24.896 --> 00:16:29.316
Now suppose a supplied hydrogenation model saturates ordinary carbon carbon

00:16:29.316 --> 00:16:33.376
multiple bonds while retaining the carbon skeleton and not opening rings.

00:16:34.316 --> 00:16:39.136
A cycloalkene with one ring and one double bond consumes one mole hydrogen per

00:16:39.136 --> 00:16:42.436
mole molecule, giving a saturated monocyclic hydrocarbon.

00:16:43.236 --> 00:16:48.856
An acyclic diene consumes two, while an acyclic alkyne also consumes two on

00:16:48.856 --> 00:16:50.296
complete reduction to the alkane.

00:16:51.076 --> 00:16:55.316
Thus two moles hydrogen uptake cannot distinguish a diene from an alkyne

00:16:55.316 --> 00:16:56.656
without additional evidence.

00:16:56.656 --> 00:17:01.216
A ring contributes to the formula based unsaturation count even when it does

00:17:01.216 --> 00:17:03.816
not consume hydrogen in the stated reaction model.

00:17:04.716 --> 00:17:09.116
Real catalyst and substrate chemistry can be more complicated, especially for

00:17:09.116 --> 00:17:13.056
strained rings or aromatic systems, so the restriction is essential.

00:17:14.056 --> 00:17:17.676
Count formula units, reactive multiple bonds and structural possibilities

00:17:17.676 --> 00:17:21.296
separately before claiming that one measurement identifies a unique

00:17:21.296 --> 00:17:21.836
hydrocarbon.

00:17:22.344 --> 00:17:27.384
A terminal alkyne can form a carbon nucleophile after its terminal proton is

00:17:27.384 --> 00:17:29.024
removed in a suitable acid base step.

00:17:30.084 --> 00:17:35.237
In the elementary acetylide alkylation model, that carbon nucleophile attacks

00:17:35.237 --> 00:17:35.304
a

00:17:35.304 --> 00:17:39.504
suitable primary alkyl halide through substitution and makes a new carbon

00:17:39.504 --> 00:17:40.164
carbon bond.

00:17:41.404 --> 00:17:44.604
Consider the anion derived from ethyne and an ethyl halide.

00:17:45.304 --> 00:17:50.240
The two carbon acetylide fragment joins the two carbon ethyl fragment, giving

00:17:50.240 --> 00:17:50.304
a

00:17:50.304 --> 00:17:53.124
four carbon alkyne, but one yne.

00:17:53.124 --> 00:17:57.764
The triple bond remains; substitution replaces the halide on the alkyl partner

00:17:58.304 --> 00:18:00.024
rather than hydrogenating the alkyne.

00:18:01.284 --> 00:18:05.304
Starting instead with the three carbon terminal alkyne propyne and a methyl

00:18:05.304 --> 00:18:10.024
halide gives the four carbon internal alkyne but two yne.

00:18:10.024 --> 00:18:13.044
The location of the triple bond follows the connectivity of the joined

00:18:13.044 --> 00:18:15.484
fragments, not just their total carbon count.

00:18:16.264 --> 00:18:20.844
Secondary and tertiary alkyl halides can favour elimination with a strongly

00:18:20.844 --> 00:18:25.044
basic acetylide, so they are not interchangeable with a suitable primary

00:18:25.044 --> 00:18:27.204
substrate in a simple selective route.

00:18:28.424 --> 00:18:30.484
Proton donors can also consume the acetylide.

00:18:31.664 --> 00:18:35.544
Treat these examples as reaction planning and carbon ledgers under the stated

00:18:35.544 --> 00:18:39.364
model, not as instructions for preparing or handling the reagent.

00:18:39.552 --> 00:18:43.932
Addition polymerization changes how monomer units are connected without

00:18:43.932 --> 00:18:47.112
requiring a water molecule to be removed from every link.

00:18:47.832 --> 00:18:52.472
In the ideal ethene example, the carbon carbon double bond opens into chain

00:18:52.472 --> 00:18:57.932
connectivity and each repeat unit retains the monomer's two carbons and four

00:18:57.932 --> 00:18:58.472
hydrogens.

00:18:59.112 --> 00:19:03.812
The repeat unit mass is approximately twenty eight grams per mole using the

00:19:03.812 --> 00:19:05.532
supplied integer atomic masses.

00:19:05.532 --> 00:19:10.917
If a hypothetical chain has molar mass twenty eight thousand grams per mole

00:19:10.917 --> 00:19:11.132
and

00:19:11.132 --> 00:19:15.332
end group masses are neglected, its degree of polymerization is about one

00:19:15.332 --> 00:19:16.392
thousand repeat units.

00:19:17.332 --> 00:19:21.372
That is not one thousand carbon atoms: the repeat units together contain about

00:19:21.372 --> 00:19:22.472
two thousand carbons.

00:19:23.372 --> 00:19:27.652
One mole of such ideal chains represents about one thousand moles of

00:19:27.652 --> 00:19:28.452
incorporated monomer units.

00:19:29.392 --> 00:19:33.312
Real polymer samples contain a distribution of chain lengths, and a reported

00:19:33.312 --> 00:19:36.732
average molar mass must be interpreted with its averaging convention.

00:19:38.372 --> 00:19:42.252
Initiator fragments, end groups, branching and unreacted monomer can affect

00:19:42.252 --> 00:19:43.392
actual mass accounting.

00:19:44.212 --> 00:19:48.112
The elementary repeat unit calculation therefore supplies an approximate chain

00:19:48.112 --> 00:19:52.932
size under stated assumptions, rather than proving that every molecule in a

00:19:52.932 --> 00:19:56.972
sample has exactly the same length or that a condensation water loss should be

00:19:56.972 --> 00:19:57.432
subtracted.

00:19:57.912 --> 00:20:03.272
A disubstituted aromatic ring requires combining the effects of both existing

00:20:03.272 --> 00:20:03.572
groups.

00:20:04.932 --> 00:20:08.881
Consider a ring numbered with a methyl group at carbon one and a carboxyl

00:20:08.881 --> 00:20:09.152
group

00:20:09.152 --> 00:20:10.112
at carbon four.

00:20:10.752 --> 00:20:14.812
In the usual introductory electrophilic substitution comparison, methyl

00:20:14.812 --> 00:20:15.212
favours

00:20:15.212 --> 00:20:17.792
ortho and para positions relative to itself.

00:20:18.612 --> 00:20:23.332
Its free ortho positions are two and six, while its para position is already

00:20:23.332 --> 00:20:23.652
occupied.

00:20:23.652 --> 00:20:29.212
The carboxyl group is a meta director, and positions two and six are also meta

00:20:29.212 --> 00:20:30.052
to carbon four.

00:20:31.032 --> 00:20:34.752
Thus both preferences point toward the same two symmetry related sites in this

00:20:34.752 --> 00:20:35.112
example.

00:20:36.092 --> 00:20:40.532
Positions two and six describe the same constitutional monoproduct after the

00:20:40.532 --> 00:20:44.392
appropriate ring symmetry is considered, rather than two guaranteed different

00:20:44.392 --> 00:20:44.692
products.

00:20:45.592 --> 00:20:49.512
This agreement does not mean the ring reacts faster than benzene overall or

00:20:49.512 --> 00:20:51.132
that all other products are impossible.

00:20:51.802 --> 00:20:55.492
The electron withdrawing group can reduce reactivity, and the observed

00:20:55.492 --> 00:20:57.252
selectivity depends on conditions.

00:20:58.132 --> 00:21:01.812
If two groups favour different sites, their relative influence and steric

00:21:01.812 --> 00:21:05.872
effects need to be considered instead of applying a mechanical one group rule.

00:21:07.212 --> 00:21:09.652
State the numbering, occupied positions and symmetry.

00:21:10.128 --> 00:21:14.868
Two alkene hydration routes can give different alcohols while retaining the

00:21:14.868 --> 00:21:16.108
same carbon skeleton.

00:21:17.168 --> 00:21:21.388
In ordinary acid catalysed hydration of propene, the hydroxyl group appears

00:21:21.388 --> 00:21:25.568
mainly on the central carbon, giving propan two ol.

00:21:25.568 --> 00:21:30.688
Hydroboration followed by suitable oxidation instead gives propan one ol,

00:21:30.688 --> 00:21:33.628
placing the hydroxyl group on the less substituted terminal carbon.

00:21:34.628 --> 00:21:38.628
During hydroboration, boron and hydrogen add from the same face of the double

00:21:38.628 --> 00:21:38.848
bond.

00:21:39.568 --> 00:21:44.168
Oxidation replaces the carbon boron bond with a carbon oxygen bond with

00:21:44.168 --> 00:21:48.568
retention at that carbon, so the overall addition of hydrogen and hydroxyl is

00:21:48.568 --> 00:21:48.868
syn.

00:21:49.448 --> 00:21:53.088
This is a stereochemical statement, while the less substituted carbon

00:21:53.088 --> 00:21:54.988
preference is a regiochemical statement.

00:21:55.988 --> 00:21:59.208
Neither should be confused with the radical peroxide effect for hydrogen

00:21:59.208 --> 00:21:59.648
bromide.

00:22:00.328 --> 00:22:04.428
The hydroboration step does not proceed through a free carbocation, so the

00:22:04.428 --> 00:22:08.268
familiar carbocation rearrangement argument is not the pathway used here.

00:22:08.848 --> 00:22:12.797
Both propene products have three carbons and the alcohol formula three

00:22:12.797 --> 00:22:13.248
carbons,

00:22:13.588 --> 00:22:15.268
eight hydrogens and one oxygen.

00:22:16.228 --> 00:22:19.708
Their different hydroxyl positions make them constitutional isomers.

00:22:20.808 --> 00:22:24.588
Always name both the reagent sequence and the product connectivity before

00:22:24.588 --> 00:22:25.888
applying an orientation label.

00:22:26.280 --> 00:22:30.320
Cleavage of an alkene does not always stop at the same products as reductive

00:22:30.320 --> 00:22:31.100
ozonolysis.

00:22:31.980 --> 00:22:36.780
Under the standard strong oxidative cleavage model using hot permanganate with

00:22:36.780 --> 00:22:40.940
an appropriate acid workup, examine each original double bonded carbon

00:22:40.940 --> 00:22:41.460
separately.

00:22:42.320 --> 00:22:46.000
A carbon carrying one hydrogen gives a carboxylic acid fragment.

00:22:46.760 --> 00:22:49.520
A carbon carrying no hydrogen gives a ketone fragment.

00:22:50.300 --> 00:22:54.180
A terminal methylene carbon can be oxidized through smaller intermediates to

00:22:54.180 --> 00:22:56.900
carbon dioxide in the complete oxidation model.

00:22:57.960 --> 00:23:02.184
Thus propene gives ethanoic acid and carbon dioxide after the specified

00:23:02.184 --> 00:23:02.600
workup,

00:23:03.180 --> 00:23:06.440
accounting for two organic carbons plus one inorganic carbon.

00:23:07.340 --> 00:23:12.360
Two methylbut two ene instead gives propanone and ethanoic acid: its more

00:23:12.360 --> 00:23:16.140
substituted double bond carbon becomes a ketone and its hydrogen bearing

00:23:16.140 --> 00:23:17.460
partner becomes an acid.

00:23:18.400 --> 00:23:20.480
The total carbon count remains five.

00:23:21.540 --> 00:23:25.840
Cold dilute permanganate under mild conditions can instead favour addition to

00:23:25.840 --> 00:23:30.100
form a vicinal diol, so the reagent name alone does not fix the oxidation

00:23:30.100 --> 00:23:30.560
extent.

00:23:31.740 --> 00:23:34.520
Carboxylate salts can be present before acid workup.

00:23:35.440 --> 00:23:39.560
State conditions and workup before choosing between diol formation, aldehyde

00:23:39.560 --> 00:23:41.700
formation and further oxidative cleavage.

00:23:42.048 --> 00:23:46.748
Hydrogenation energy can compare two starting alkenes only when the reaction

00:23:46.748 --> 00:23:48.448
endpoints are properly matched.

00:23:49.408 --> 00:23:53.734
Suppose two isomeric alkenes each consume one mole of hydrogen to form the

00:23:53.734 --> 00:23:53.968
same

00:23:53.968 --> 00:23:55.708
alkane in the same physical state.

00:23:56.628 --> 00:24:00.343
Use illustrative hydrogenation enthalpies of minus one hundred twenty

00:24:00.343 --> 00:24:03.948
kilojoules per mole for A and minus one hundred sixteen for B.

00:24:04.608 --> 00:24:08.968
Because both routes finish at the same enthalpy, A must start four kilojoules

00:24:08.968 --> 00:24:10.108
per mole above B.

00:24:10.488 --> 00:24:15.148
It releases more heat because its initial enthalpy is higher, so B is the more

00:24:15.148 --> 00:24:17.408
enthalpically stable alkene in this comparison.

00:24:18.428 --> 00:24:22.248
The less negative hydrogenation enthalpy therefore corresponds to the lower

00:24:22.248 --> 00:24:24.428
starting enthalpy when the endpoints match.

00:24:25.348 --> 00:24:29.648
This does not establish which alkene hydrogenates faster: activation barriers

00:24:29.648 --> 00:24:30.748
control reaction rates.

00:24:31.448 --> 00:24:35.328
It also does not directly provide an equilibrium constant, which depends on

00:24:35.328 --> 00:24:37.428
Gibbs energy and therefore includes entropy.

00:24:38.308 --> 00:24:42.108
Comparing different product alkanes without correcting their enthalpies would

00:24:42.108 --> 00:24:43.668
invalidate the simple subtraction.

00:24:44.348 --> 00:24:49.028
In exam data, first verify equal reaction stoichiometry and a common final

00:24:49.028 --> 00:24:53.068
product, then subtract the signed enthalpies before interpreting stability.

00:24:53.496 --> 00:24:57.476
The Wurtz reaction illustrates why a carbon joining reaction is not

00:24:57.476 --> 00:24:59.176
automatically a selective synthesis.

00:25:00.016 --> 00:25:04.136
In the simple net model, two alkyl bromide molecules react with two sodium

00:25:04.136 --> 00:25:08.116
atoms to give one coupled alkane and two sodium bromide units.

00:25:08.936 --> 00:25:13.136
With only a suitable primary ethyl halide, the carbon groups can join to give

00:25:13.136 --> 00:25:13.576
butane.

00:25:14.376 --> 00:25:17.076
Now consider a mixture of methyl and ethyl halides.

00:25:17.916 --> 00:25:22.436
Methyl with methyl gives ethane, methyl with ethyl gives propane, and ethyl

00:25:22.436 --> 00:25:23.756
with ethyl gives butane.

00:25:24.636 --> 00:25:28.256
The cross coupled product is therefore accompanied by two possible self

00:25:28.256 --> 00:25:29.136
coupling skeletons.

00:25:30.336 --> 00:25:33.496
Listing those three products does not prove that they occur in equal amounts,

00:25:33.496 --> 00:25:36.236
or that the desired cross product has a fixed yield.

00:25:37.256 --> 00:25:41.076
Reactivity, competing reactions and conditions affect the actual mixture.

00:25:41.876 --> 00:25:45.336
The mixed route is consequently a poor general way to claim selective

00:25:45.336 --> 00:25:47.376
preparation of an unsymmetrical alkane.

00:25:48.256 --> 00:25:52.621
Carbon accounting still provides a reliable check: combine the carbon counts

00:25:52.621 --> 00:25:52.736
of

00:25:52.736 --> 00:25:56.496
the two groups that actually join, rather than adding all the starting carbons

00:25:56.496 --> 00:25:57.436
into every product.

00:25:58.476 --> 00:26:02.036
Treat the net equation as transformation bookkeeping and keep selectivity

00:26:02.036 --> 00:26:03.896
separate from theoretical stoichiometry.

00:26:04.464 --> 00:26:08.964
Stereospecific addition can preserve information about an alkene's starting

00:26:08.964 --> 00:26:09.484
geometry.

00:26:10.484 --> 00:26:14.544
Consider the usual bromonium mediated anti addition to but two ene in a

00:26:14.544 --> 00:26:15.944
suitable non nucleophilic medium.

00:26:16.644 --> 00:26:20.724
The trans alkene gives meso two three dibromobutane, with opposite

00:26:20.724 --> 00:26:24.344
configurations at its two stereogenic carbons and internal symmetry.

00:26:25.044 --> 00:26:30.224
The cis alkene instead gives the two enantiomeric configurations two R three R

00:26:30.224 --> 00:26:34.204
and two S three S in equal amounts under achiral conditions.

00:26:34.464 --> 00:26:38.924
The meso product and the racemic mixture can both show zero net optical

00:26:38.924 --> 00:26:40.844
rotation, but for different reasons.

00:26:41.664 --> 00:26:46.324
A meso molecule is achiral despite containing stereogenic centres; a racemic

00:26:46.324 --> 00:26:49.384
sample contains chiral molecules whose rotations cancel.

00:26:50.204 --> 00:26:54.064
Do not identify these outcomes merely from the word anti, since chain rotation

00:26:54.064 --> 00:26:57.024
can change a product drawing without changing its configuration.

00:26:58.044 --> 00:27:01.524
Track the initial alkene geometry and the three dimensional addition together.

00:27:02.124 --> 00:27:06.564
Both transformations retain four carbons and add two bromines, giving the same

00:27:06.584 --> 00:27:08.164
constitutional dibromide formula.

00:27:08.924 --> 00:27:13.184
A competing nucleophile such as water can change the functional product, so

00:27:13.184 --> 00:27:15.904
this comparison assumes the stated dibromide pathway.

00:27:16.224 --> 00:27:21.384
Strong oxidative cleavage of an alkyne does not give the same products as its

00:27:21.384 --> 00:27:22.224
ordinary hydration.

00:27:23.204 --> 00:27:27.604
In a supplied cleavage model, each carbon of an internal triple bond becomes a

00:27:27.604 --> 00:27:31.464
carboxyl carbon in one fragment after the stated acidic workup.

00:27:32.524 --> 00:27:37.024
Pent two yne therefore gives ethanoic acid with two carbons and propanoic acid

00:27:37.024 --> 00:27:37.504
with three.

00:27:37.924 --> 00:27:42.624
The triple bond divided the five carbon chain into those two pieces; no extra

00:27:42.624 --> 00:27:43.824
carbon entered with oxygen.

00:27:44.424 --> 00:27:48.664
For pent one yne, the terminal triple bond carbon instead reaches carbon

00:27:48.664 --> 00:27:53.144
dioxide in the complete oxidation model, while the other fragment is four

00:27:53.144 --> 00:27:54.324
carbon butanoic acid.

00:27:55.424 --> 00:27:58.924
Counting only the isolated organic acid would miss one starting carbon.

00:27:59.964 --> 00:28:03.804
Compare these outcomes with a hydration map, which retains all five carbons in

00:28:03.804 --> 00:28:06.504
one carbonyl product rather than cleaving the skeleton.

00:28:07.504 --> 00:28:11.944
Actual oxidation can be more complex and yields need not be quantitative, so

00:28:11.944 --> 00:28:14.964
the named products belong to the stated ideal cleavage endpoint.

00:28:16.144 --> 00:28:19.749
Mark both original triple bond carbons before naming the fragments, and

00:28:19.749 --> 00:28:20.104
include

00:28:20.104 --> 00:28:23.344
inorganic carbon products when checking total carbon conservation.

00:28:23.736 --> 00:28:28.228
Aromatic sulfonation is a useful exception to treating every substitution

00:28:28.228 --> 00:28:28.536
arrow

00:28:28.536 --> 00:28:29.596
as irreversible.

00:28:30.376 --> 00:28:34.816
In the conceptual benzene and sulfur trioxide map, a ring hydrogen is replaced

00:28:34.816 --> 00:28:39.016
by a sulfonic acid group while the six carbon framework remains intact.

00:28:40.336 --> 00:28:44.336
Sulfur trioxide and strongly acidic sulfonating media favour the forward

00:28:44.336 --> 00:28:48.896
transformation; suitable hot dilute aqueous acidic conditions can favour

00:28:48.896 --> 00:28:51.876
removal of the sulfonic group and return a ring hydrogen.

00:28:52.836 --> 00:28:57.200
The overall direction depends on the specified medium and equilibrium, not

00:28:57.200 --> 00:28:57.436
just

00:28:57.436 --> 00:28:59.076
on recognizing a named reaction.

00:29:00.216 --> 00:29:03.964
A sulfonic acid group deactivates the ring and is usually meta directing

00:29:03.964 --> 00:29:04.276
toward

00:29:04.276 --> 00:29:06.016
further electrophilic substitution.

00:29:06.896 --> 00:29:11.196
It can also occupy a position temporarily in a supporting synthesis plan, but

00:29:11.196 --> 00:29:13.696
removal requires a separate stated transformation.

00:29:14.916 --> 00:29:18.816
Do not confuse sulfonation with attaching an alkyl group or with a sulfone

00:29:18.816 --> 00:29:20.756
containing two carbon groups on sulfur.

00:29:21.446 --> 00:29:26.316
The formula benzene plus sulfur trioxide balances without losing carbon or

00:29:26.316 --> 00:29:27.456
generating an extra ring.

00:29:28.336 --> 00:29:32.496
This is a functional group and reversibility comparison, not a set of

00:29:32.496 --> 00:29:35.796
experimental preparation conditions or a promise that every substituted

00:29:35.796 --> 00:29:38.596
aromatic substrate follows the same selective pathway.

00:29:38.904 --> 00:29:43.464
Supplied gas volume data can identify a molecular formula under carefully

00:29:43.464 --> 00:29:44.444
stated assumptions.

00:29:45.684 --> 00:29:49.689
Consider twenty millilitres of one pure gaseous hydrocarbon mixed with one

00:29:49.689 --> 00:29:51.204
hundred thirty millilitres of oxygen.

00:29:52.264 --> 00:29:55.104
Complete combustion is followed by cooling so water condenses.

00:29:55.984 --> 00:29:59.644
At the same comparison temperature and pressure, the remaining gas volume is

00:29:59.644 --> 00:30:01.204
ninety millilitres.

00:30:01.204 --> 00:30:05.364
A separate selective carbon dioxide absorption leaves thirty millilitres of

00:30:05.364 --> 00:30:05.504
oxygen.

00:30:06.384 --> 00:30:11.964
Thus carbon dioxide occupied sixty millilitres and oxygen consumed was one

00:30:11.964 --> 00:30:12.704
hundred.

00:30:12.704 --> 00:30:16.544
Dividing by the original hydrocarbon volume gives three carbon dioxide volumes

00:30:16.544 --> 00:30:19.404
and five oxygen volumes per hydrocarbon volume.

00:30:20.524 --> 00:30:25.764
In the equation for carbon x hydrogen y, carbon dioxide coefficient is x and

00:30:25.764 --> 00:30:28.344
oxygen coefficient is x plus y over four.

00:30:29.364 --> 00:30:33.604
Therefore x is three and y is eight: the supplied hydrocarbon is propane.

00:30:34.384 --> 00:30:38.724
The cooled ninety millilitres is not pure carbon dioxide because excess oxygen

00:30:38.724 --> 00:30:39.184
remains.

00:30:39.924 --> 00:30:44.484
The calculation assumes only the stated gases, complete combustion, selective

00:30:44.484 --> 00:30:47.584
absorption and negligible residual water vapour.

00:30:47.584 --> 00:30:52.104
It is interpretation of idealized observations, not an instruction to ignite a

00:30:52.104 --> 00:30:52.384
gas mixture.

00:30:52.608 --> 00:30:56.488
The more reactive type of hydrogen does not automatically supply the product

00:30:56.488 --> 00:30:57.568
ratio by itself.

00:30:58.548 --> 00:31:01.928
Consider an original low conversion monochlorination model for propane.

00:31:02.808 --> 00:31:07.388
Its two equivalent methyl ends contain six primary hydrogens in total, and its

00:31:07.388 --> 00:31:10.108
middle carbon contains two secondary hydrogens.

00:31:11.148 --> 00:31:14.848
Suppose the supplied relative abstraction rate per secondary hydrogen is four,

00:31:15.488 --> 00:31:19.108
while that per primary hydrogen is one under the stated conditions.

00:31:19.828 --> 00:31:24.408
Multiply the number of each type of hydrogen by its per hydrogen reactivity.

00:31:24.648 --> 00:31:28.268
The primary substitution channel has weight six times one, or six.

00:31:28.868 --> 00:31:32.088
The secondary channel has weight two times four, or eight.

00:31:32.808 --> 00:31:37.168
Normalize by the total fourteen to obtain three sevenths one chloropropane and

00:31:37.168 --> 00:31:41.715
four sevenths two chloropropane, approximately forty two point nine and fifty

00:31:41.715 --> 00:31:42.948
seven point one percent.

00:31:43.168 --> 00:31:47.088
The two equivalent chain ends give the same primary constitutional product.

00:31:47.608 --> 00:31:51.788
Neither the raw six to two hydrogen count nor the bare one to four reactivity

00:31:51.788 --> 00:31:53.888
ratio is the final product ratio.

00:31:54.708 --> 00:31:58.568
The value four is supplied for this exercise, not a universal chlorination

00:31:58.568 --> 00:31:59.068
constant.

00:31:59.868 --> 00:32:03.908
Repeated substitution, changing conditions and substantial conversion can

00:32:03.908 --> 00:32:05.728
invalidate the simple two channel estimate.

00:32:06.024 --> 00:32:10.064
Two reactions that add groups to the same double bond can produce different

00:32:10.064 --> 00:32:13.464
stereochemical outcomes because their addition geometries differ.

00:32:14.324 --> 00:32:19.184
Use an ideal stereospecific syn hydroxylation of but two ene, meaning both

00:32:19.184 --> 00:32:23.184
oxygen groups are delivered from the same alkene face and then appear as

00:32:23.184 --> 00:32:25.984
adjacent hydroxyl groups after the stated workup.

00:32:26.284 --> 00:32:30.364
The carbon skeleton stays intact and the product is butane two three diol.

00:32:31.064 --> 00:32:35.464
Starting from the cis alkene gives the meso configuration, with opposite R and

00:32:35.464 --> 00:32:41.004
S centres; its alternative R S and S R labels describe the same molecule under

00:32:41.004 --> 00:32:42.084
the end to end symmetry.

00:32:43.204 --> 00:32:47.884
Starting from the trans alkene gives the R R and S S enantiomer pair when both

00:32:47.884 --> 00:32:50.324
faces react equally under achiral conditions.

00:32:51.124 --> 00:32:54.904
Compare this with the earlier anti bromination map: the stereochemical

00:32:54.904 --> 00:32:56.244
relationship is reversed.

00:32:56.744 --> 00:33:00.994
Do not transfer the word meso from one reaction to another without checking

00:33:00.994 --> 00:33:01.164
syn

00:33:01.164 --> 00:33:04.024
versus anti delivery and the actual substituents.

00:33:04.984 --> 00:33:09.424
Both a meso sample and an exactly racemic sample can have zero net optical

00:33:09.424 --> 00:33:10.904
rotation for different reasons.

00:33:11.664 --> 00:33:15.684
These statements assume the specified stereospecific pathway, an achiral

00:33:15.684 --> 00:33:20.544
environment and no later stereochemical scrambling; they are not a claim that

00:33:20.544 --> 00:33:23.104
every oxidizing condition gives an intact diol.

00:33:23.280 --> 00:33:27.200
Breaking a double bond does not always split an organic molecule into two

00:33:27.200 --> 00:33:27.920
separate molecules.

00:33:28.780 --> 00:33:32.588
Consider cyclohexene with one double bond between labelled ring carbons one

00:33:32.588 --> 00:33:32.740
and

00:33:32.740 --> 00:33:33.060
two.

00:33:33.800 --> 00:33:38.140
In reductive ozonolysis, remove the bond between those two carbons and replace

00:33:38.140 --> 00:33:42.020
each double bonded carbon by a carbonyl carbon while retaining its other

00:33:42.020 --> 00:33:43.560
original neighbours.

00:33:43.560 --> 00:33:47.983
Each alkene carbon originally bears a hydrogen, so each becomes an aldehyde

00:33:47.983 --> 00:33:48.160
end

00:33:48.160 --> 00:33:49.960
under the stated reductive workup.

00:33:49.960 --> 00:33:54.460
The remaining path through carbons three, four, five and six still connects

00:33:54.460 --> 00:33:54.640
the

00:33:54.640 --> 00:33:56.160
two former double bond carbons.

00:33:57.140 --> 00:34:01.540
The product is therefore a single six carbon chain with an aldehyde at each

00:34:01.540 --> 00:34:07.440
end: hexane one six dial, written O H C, four methylene groups, then C H

00:34:07.440 --> 00:34:07.860
O.

00:34:07.860 --> 00:34:11.520
Its formula is carbon six hydrogen ten oxygen two.

00:34:12.120 --> 00:34:15.300
One mole of the starting monocyclic alkene gives one mole

00:34:15.300 --> 00:34:19.898
of this dialdehyde in the ideal quantitative map, not two moles of three

00:34:19.898 --> 00:34:20.920
carbon aldehyde.

00:34:20.920 --> 00:34:24.000
The ring has opened, but the carbon inventory remains six.

00:34:24.980 --> 00:34:29.060
Further oxidation can convert aldehyde ends to acids, so the workup must be

00:34:29.060 --> 00:34:29.340
stated.

00:34:30.140 --> 00:34:33.900
More complicated polycyclic substrates require tracing every remaining carbon

00:34:33.900 --> 00:34:37.280
connection rather than applying a universal two fragment rule.

00:34:37.536 --> 00:34:41.642
An orientation rule for proton addition is not the last step when a

00:34:41.642 --> 00:34:42.316
carbocation

00:34:42.316 --> 00:34:43.136
can rearrange.

00:34:44.116 --> 00:34:47.996
Use three methylbut one ene with its four carbon chain labelled one through

00:34:47.996 --> 00:34:50.356
four and a methyl branch at carbon three.

00:34:51.196 --> 00:34:55.016
In a conventional ionic hydrogen chloride addition description, proton

00:34:55.016 --> 00:34:59.437
attachment at terminal carbon one leaves a secondary carbocation at carbon

00:34:59.437 --> 00:34:59.676
two.

00:35:00.576 --> 00:35:04.276
A neighbouring carbon three hydrogen can migrate with its bond pair to carbon

00:35:04.276 --> 00:35:08.398
two, moving the positive site to carbon three and giving a tertiary

00:35:08.398 --> 00:35:09.136
carbocation.

00:35:10.176 --> 00:35:14.276
Chloride capture there yields the rearranged connectivity two chloro two

00:35:14.276 --> 00:35:16.576
methylbutane after renumbering the product.

00:35:17.836 --> 00:35:22.163
Direct capture before the shift instead gives the unrearranged connectivity

00:35:22.163 --> 00:35:22.336
two

00:35:22.336 --> 00:35:23.556
chloro three methylbutane.

00:35:24.296 --> 00:35:28.277
Both retain five carbons and have formula carbon five hydrogen eleven

00:35:28.277 --> 00:35:28.796
chlorine.

00:35:29.576 --> 00:35:33.896
Their relative amounts depend on competing pathways; a stability comparison

00:35:33.896 --> 00:35:37.276
alone does not justify declaring a single quantitative product.

00:35:38.156 --> 00:35:42.976
This is a specified ionic H C l example, not the peroxide initiated hydrogen

00:35:42.976 --> 00:35:44.076
bromide chain mechanism.

00:35:45.076 --> 00:35:49.336
Track the labelled carbons through protonation, hydrogen migration and capture

00:35:49.336 --> 00:35:51.036
before renumbering the final name.

00:35:51.396 --> 00:35:56.111
The migrating hydrogen carries an electron pair; no carbon atom or free

00:35:56.111 --> 00:35:56.576
hydride

00:35:56.576 --> 00:35:57.536
reagent is inserted.

00:35:58.440 --> 00:36:02.500
A desired alkylbenzene skeleton should be checked against the electrophile

00:36:02.500 --> 00:36:05.980
pathway, not inferred from the starting halide name alone.

00:36:06.820 --> 00:36:11.070
In the usual selected Friedel Crafts comparison, direct benzene alkylation

00:36:11.070 --> 00:36:11.300
with

00:36:11.300 --> 00:36:16.540
a normal propyl halide can favor rearranged isopropylbenzene because a more

00:36:16.540 --> 00:36:20.380
stable secondary carbocation like electrophile becomes accessible.

00:36:20.840 --> 00:36:24.920
This is not a guarantee of a single pure product for every condition.

00:36:25.860 --> 00:36:31.020
An alternative route introduces a propanoyl group by acylation, giving benzene

00:36:31.020 --> 00:36:34.300
bonded to carbonyl carbon, then methylene and methyl.

00:36:35.080 --> 00:36:39.120
Suitable carbonyl deoxygenation converts that carbonyl carbon to a methylene

00:36:39.120 --> 00:36:44.540
while retaining the three carbon side chain, producing normal propylbenzene.

00:36:44.540 --> 00:36:48.660
Both final alkylbenzene skeletons have nine total carbons, but their

00:36:48.660 --> 00:36:49.660
connectivity differs.

00:36:50.240 --> 00:36:54.920
The acylium type electrophile does not undergo the same ordinary alkyl

00:36:54.920 --> 00:36:56.300
carbocation rearrangement.

00:36:57.300 --> 00:37:01.020
Reagent compatibility still matters when removing the carbonyl, especially if

00:37:01.020 --> 00:37:02.420
other sensitive groups are present.

00:37:03.140 --> 00:37:07.352
This is a conceptual reaction map rather than a promise that any reducing

00:37:07.352 --> 00:37:07.640
agent

00:37:07.640 --> 00:37:08.460
removes oxygen.

00:37:08.460 --> 00:37:12.978
Follow the carbonyl carbon into the final benzylic carbon before naming the

00:37:12.978 --> 00:37:13.460
product.

00:37:14.040 --> 00:37:18.360
The first aromatic substitution changes the substrate that faces the next

00:37:18.360 --> 00:37:19.020
electrophile.

00:37:19.720 --> 00:37:24.240
An alkyl group generally donates electron density and activates a benzene ring

00:37:24.240 --> 00:37:26.280
toward further electrophilic substitution.

00:37:27.200 --> 00:37:32.220
A newly formed alkylbenzene can therefore react again, which helps explain why

00:37:32.220 --> 00:37:36.560
Friedel Crafts alkylation may give repeated substitution rather than cleanly

00:37:36.560 --> 00:37:38.060
stopping after one added group.

00:37:38.060 --> 00:37:42.920
An acyl group has a carbonyl attached to the ring and withdraws electron

00:37:42.920 --> 00:37:44.420
density in the usual comparison.

00:37:45.240 --> 00:37:49.471
The resulting acylbenzene is less reactive toward installation of a second

00:37:49.471 --> 00:37:49.700
acyl

00:37:49.700 --> 00:37:52.100
group under ordinary Friedel Crafts conditions.

00:37:52.960 --> 00:37:56.220
This suppresses repeat introduction of a carbonyl containing substituent.

00:37:56.940 --> 00:38:00.960
This difference is about ring reactivity as well as the earlier distinction in

00:38:00.960 --> 00:38:02.000
skeletal rearrangement.

00:38:02.000 --> 00:38:07.320
Strongly deactivated substrates such as nitrobenzene do not normally undergo

00:38:07.320 --> 00:38:11.569
the standard Friedel Crafts reaction merely because an alkyl halide and a

00:38:11.569 --> 00:38:11.860
Lewis

00:38:11.860 --> 00:38:12.580
acid are named.

00:38:13.600 --> 00:38:16.860
Catalyst binding by other functional groups can add another limitation.

00:38:17.900 --> 00:38:21.801
Directing effects, activation and isolated yield are separate pieces of

00:38:21.801 --> 00:38:22.460
information.

00:38:23.200 --> 00:38:27.520
Do not infer an exact product percentage from the word activating, or assume

00:38:27.520 --> 00:38:31.200
the same reaction map applies to every substituted aromatic compound.

00:38:31.608 --> 00:38:36.468
Before applying directing preferences, identify which aromatic positions are

00:38:36.468 --> 00:38:38.468
constitutionally equivalent by symmetry.

00:38:39.628 --> 00:38:44.228
Consider formal ring mononitration of the three xylene isomers, replacing one

00:38:44.228 --> 00:38:47.988
remaining ring hydrogen with a nitro group while leaving the two identical

00:38:47.988 --> 00:38:50.528
methyl groups fixed as a substitution pattern.

00:38:51.168 --> 00:38:56.088
In ortho xylene, positions three and six are equivalent, while four and five

00:38:56.088 --> 00:38:57.688
form a second equivalent pair.

00:38:58.148 --> 00:39:01.028
There are two symmetry distinct constitutional placements.

00:39:01.988 --> 00:39:06.548
In meta xylene, position two is unique, positions four and six are equivalent,

00:39:06.908 --> 00:39:10.308
and position five is another unique site, giving three patterns.

00:39:11.388 --> 00:39:15.708
In para xylene, all four remaining ring positions are equivalent, giving one

00:39:15.708 --> 00:39:15.928
pattern.

00:39:16.728 --> 00:39:20.308
These are counts of distinct placements, not a statement that every placement

00:39:20.308 --> 00:39:24.564
is formed in an equal amount or is readily isolated under one chosen set of

00:39:24.564 --> 00:39:25.188
conditions.

00:39:26.548 --> 00:39:30.308
Electronic directing preferences and steric effects influence actual product

00:39:30.308 --> 00:39:30.908
proportions.

00:39:32.168 --> 00:39:35.968
Rotating or reflecting the same substituted ring drawing must not create a new

00:39:35.968 --> 00:39:36.988
constitutional product.

00:39:38.448 --> 00:39:42.148
State that side chain substitution and further nitration are excluded from

00:39:42.148 --> 00:39:42.348
this

00:39:42.348 --> 00:39:42.788
counting problem.

00:39:43.824 --> 00:39:47.984
Hydrogenation data can illustrate aromatic stabilization only when the

00:39:47.984 --> 00:39:49.264
reference comparison is stated.

00:39:50.144 --> 00:39:53.784
Use an illustrative isolated double bond benchmark of minus one hundred twenty

00:39:53.784 --> 00:39:54.984
kilojoules per mole.

00:39:56.224 --> 00:39:59.684
Assign a hypothetical localized three double bond structure the additive

00:39:59.684 --> 00:40:03.584
estimate three times that value, or minus three hundred sixty, for

00:40:03.584 --> 00:40:05.664
hydrogenation to one mole of cyclohexane.

00:40:07.024 --> 00:40:10.631
Compare benzene hydrogenation to that same product state using minus two

00:40:10.631 --> 00:40:11.884
hundred eight kilojoules per mole.

00:40:12.204 --> 00:40:15.144
The difference is one hundred fifty two kilojoules per mole.

00:40:16.424 --> 00:40:20.264
Within this benchmark construction, benzene begins lower in enthalpy than the

00:40:20.264 --> 00:40:24.144
hypothetical localized reference, so it releases less heat on reaching the

00:40:24.144 --> 00:40:24.684
common endpoint.

00:40:25.404 --> 00:40:28.364
This is not a direct measurement of a freely existing localized

00:40:28.364 --> 00:40:32.944
cyclohexatriene, nor a universal bond energy that can be transferred unchanged

00:40:32.944 --> 00:40:34.324
to every aromatic molecule.

00:40:35.224 --> 00:40:37.424
Different bond environments and reference choices matter.

00:40:37.824 --> 00:40:41.704
The comparison also does not give a reaction rate or a Gibbs equilibrium

00:40:41.704 --> 00:40:42.184
constant.

00:40:43.024 --> 00:40:46.424
Count the three hydrogen molecules required by benzene and keep the product

00:40:46.424 --> 00:40:49.324
amount and phase matched before interpreting the enthalpy difference.

00:40:49.584 --> 00:40:55.244
Adding one hydrogen bromide unit to a conjugated diene need not behave like an

00:40:55.244 --> 00:40:57.644
isolated double bond with no neighbouring pi system.

00:40:58.924 --> 00:41:02.984
Label the four carbons of buta one three diene consecutively for the starting

00:41:02.984 --> 00:41:03.624
atom map.

00:41:04.504 --> 00:41:08.861
In a selected ionic addition, protonation at carbon one gives an allylic

00:41:08.861 --> 00:41:09.224
cation

00:41:09.224 --> 00:41:13.584
whose positive charge is delocalized over carbon two and carbon four.

00:41:14.464 --> 00:41:18.904
Bromide capture at carbon two gives the mapped one two addition product;

00:41:19.404 --> 00:41:23.564
capture at carbon four gives the mapped one four product and leaves the double

00:41:23.564 --> 00:41:25.004
bond between the middle carbons.

00:41:25.964 --> 00:41:30.164
The constitutional products can be named three bromobut one ene and one

00:41:30.164 --> 00:41:31.224
bromobut two ene.

00:41:31.984 --> 00:41:34.644
Both have four carbons and one remaining double bond.

00:41:35.344 --> 00:41:39.304
The internal alkene product can also have geometric isomers, which a simple

00:41:39.304 --> 00:41:41.044
connectivity count does not distinguish.

00:41:41.814 --> 00:41:46.644
The labels one two and one four refer to the original diene atom map, not

00:41:46.644 --> 00:41:48.444
necessarily the final naming direction.

00:41:49.684 --> 00:41:53.684
Kinetic and thermodynamic control compare rates of formation and relative

00:41:53.684 --> 00:41:55.864
product stability under specified conditions.

00:41:56.984 --> 00:42:01.464
Temperature, time and reversibility matter; a remembered cold or hot label is

00:42:01.464 --> 00:42:05.464
not a universal prediction of a pure product or a fixed numerical ratio.

00:42:06.048 --> 00:42:10.088
An alkene can give different brominated product families depending on the

00:42:10.088 --> 00:42:10.808
reaction pathway.

00:42:11.468 --> 00:42:16.888
In a selected radical allylic bromination model, but one ene loses a hydrogen

00:42:16.888 --> 00:42:20.228
from the carbon next to its double bond and gains a bromine atom.

00:42:20.888 --> 00:42:25.348
The allylic radical has resonance contributors with the unpaired electron at

00:42:25.348 --> 00:42:27.608
different ends of the three carbon allylic fragment.

00:42:28.788 --> 00:42:34.011
Capture can therefore produce three bromobut one ene or one bromobut two ene

00:42:34.011 --> 00:42:34.148
as

00:42:34.148 --> 00:42:35.388
constitutional possibilities.

00:42:35.968 --> 00:42:39.368
A double bond remains in each mono substitution product.

00:42:40.148 --> 00:42:45.928
Their formula is C four H seven Br, showing replacement of one hydrogen rather

00:42:45.928 --> 00:42:47.448
than addition of two bromine atoms.

00:42:48.328 --> 00:42:52.808
In contrast, ordinary bromine addition across the starting double bond gives a

00:42:52.808 --> 00:42:57.600
vicinal dibromide with formula C four H eight Br two in the simplified

00:42:57.600 --> 00:42:58.148
addition

00:42:58.148 --> 00:42:58.428
model.

00:42:59.268 --> 00:43:03.408
N bromosuccinimide is commonly associated with the radical allylic pathway

00:43:03.408 --> 00:43:07.848
under suitable conditions, but a reagent label alone is not a complete

00:43:07.848 --> 00:43:09.748
mechanism or selectivity guarantee.

00:43:10.548 --> 00:43:14.568
The listed constitutional products can have additional stereochemical forms,

00:43:14.748 --> 00:43:18.508
and resonance drawings do not imply equal product amounts.

00:43:19.488 --> 00:43:23.288
Track the hydrogen inventory and residual pi bond before deciding whether

00:43:23.288 --> 00:43:26.168
bromine was introduced by substitution or addition.

00:43:26.448 --> 00:43:30.441
A lower energy conformation need not account for the whole molecular

00:43:30.441 --> 00:43:31.028
population

00:43:31.028 --> 00:43:32.108
at finite temperature.

00:43:33.048 --> 00:43:36.508
Use a supporting three state model inspired by the anti and two gauche

00:43:36.508 --> 00:43:37.628
arrangements of an alkane.

00:43:38.628 --> 00:43:42.468
Assign the anti state zero relative molar energy and each of the two gauche

00:43:42.468 --> 00:43:45.308
states a supplied energy of three thousand joules per mole.

00:43:46.108 --> 00:43:49.628
At three hundred kelvin, with the gas constant eight point three one four

00:43:49.628 --> 00:43:53.748
joules per mole per kelvin, the Boltzmann weight of each gauche state relative

00:43:53.748 --> 00:43:58.488
to anti is about zero point three zero zero four.

00:43:58.488 --> 00:44:02.068
The total weight is one plus twice that number, because there are two distinct

00:44:02.068 --> 00:44:02.688
gauche states.

00:44:03.428 --> 00:44:07.856
Normalization gives about sixty two point four seven percent anti and eighteen

00:44:07.856 --> 00:44:10.208
point seven six percent in each gauche state.

00:44:10.528 --> 00:44:15.288
The combined gauche population is therefore thirty seven point five three

00:44:15.288 --> 00:44:15.748
percent.

00:44:15.748 --> 00:44:19.237
Omitting the second gauche state would produce a different and incorrect

00:44:19.237 --> 00:44:19.528
answer

00:44:19.528 --> 00:44:20.128
for this model.

00:44:21.128 --> 00:44:24.828
The energy gap here is supplied practice data, not a universal experimental

00:44:24.828 --> 00:44:26.428
value for butane in every medium.

00:44:28.008 --> 00:44:31.468
Other conformations and unequal vibrational or rotational contributions are

00:44:31.468 --> 00:44:31.768
neglected.

00:44:33.328 --> 00:44:37.388
The model compares populations at equilibrium, not the rate of crossing a

00:44:37.388 --> 00:44:37.668
rotational barrier.

00:44:37.896 --> 00:44:43.416
Calling a terminal alkyne acidic is not enough to decide whether any chosen

00:44:43.416 --> 00:44:46.676
base will produce a useful acetylide population.

00:44:48.036 --> 00:44:51.496
Write the proton transfer from the terminal alkyne to a base anion.

00:44:52.256 --> 00:44:55.856
Its products are the acetylide and the conjugate acid of that base.

00:44:56.616 --> 00:45:01.975
In a supplied same medium comparison, take the alkyne acid constant exponent

00:45:01.975 --> 00:45:02.116
as

00:45:02.116 --> 00:45:03.316
twenty five.

00:45:03.316 --> 00:45:08.196
If the conjugate acid of base one has the supplied exponent thirty five, the

00:45:08.196 --> 00:45:13.016
transfer constant is ten to the power thirty five minus twenty five, or ten to

00:45:13.016 --> 00:45:13.396
the ten.

00:45:14.296 --> 00:45:18.576
If base two instead has conjugate acid exponent fifteen, the corresponding

00:45:18.576 --> 00:45:20.596
constant is ten to the minus ten.

00:45:21.276 --> 00:45:25.696
The first comparison strongly favours products and the second reactants under

00:45:25.696 --> 00:45:27.516
their respective standard state conventions.

00:45:27.516 --> 00:45:32.776
These are hypothetical internally consistent data, not permission to mix acid

00:45:32.776 --> 00:45:34.796
constant tables from unrelated solvents.

00:45:35.896 --> 00:45:39.336
Actual conversion still depends on starting composition and competing

00:45:39.336 --> 00:45:39.956
reactions.

00:45:41.056 --> 00:45:45.476
A base may also be consumed by water or another proton donor before the

00:45:45.476 --> 00:45:46.836
intended carbon nucleophile forms.

00:45:47.816 --> 00:45:52.136
An internal alkyne lacking a terminal carbon hydrogen bond cannot undergo this

00:45:52.136 --> 00:45:53.816
same terminal deprotonation step.

00:45:54.636 --> 00:45:58.656
Separate thermodynamic base selection from the later substitution conditions

00:45:58.656 --> 00:46:00.836
used to build a new carbon carbon bond.
